.TH TUI_YESNO_INIT 3 2026-11-15 "libtuikit 0.8" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_yesno_init, tui_yesno_handle, tui_yesno_get, tui_yesno_render, tui_yesno_format \-
the inline one-row yes/no question
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/yesno.h>
.PP
.BI "void tui_yesno_init(tui_yesno *" yn ", const char *" text );
.BI "tui_yesno_result tui_yesno_handle(tui_yesno *" yn ", const tui_event *" ev );
.BI "tui_yesno_result tui_yesno_get(const tui_yesno *" yn );
.BI "void tui_yesno_render(const tui_yesno *" yn ", tui_canvas " c ", int " row );
.BI "int  tui_yesno_format(const tui_yesno *" yn ", int " cols ", char *" out ", size_t " outsz );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tui_yesno
is the question a program asks on one row of the screen, usually the bottom
one (\(lqDelete R01? (y/N)\(rq), and answers with one key.
It is the one-row form of the modal
.BR tui_dialog_confirm (): the screen stays as it is and nothing is drawn
over it.
It is a pure model: the program gives it the events while it is open, draws
it, and reads the outcome.
.PP
.BR tui_yesno_init ()
opens the question
.IR text ;
the widget adds the suffix \(lq(y/N)\(rq.
Controls in the text are blanks; more than 255 bytes are cut at a character.
.PP
.BR tui_yesno_handle ()
takes one event and returns the outcome so far:
.TP
.B TUI_YESNO_YES
the key y or Y (without Ctrl or Alt);
.TP
.B TUI_YESNO_INTERRUPT
Ctrl-C, or the terminal hanging up: the program is expected to leave;
.TP
.B TUI_YESNO_NO
any other key, Esc included (the capital N of the suffix is the default);
.TP
.B TUI_YESNO_OPEN
a paste, a resize or another event that is not a key.
.PP
Once answered, the outcome stays.
.BR tui_yesno_get ()
reads it again.
.PP
.BR tui_yesno_render ()
draws the question on row
.I row
of the canvas, over its whole width, in the role
.B text
with the suffix in bold; while the question is open the terminal cursor is
placed after the suffix.
A question wider than the row is cut with an ellipsis so that the suffix stays;
a row narrower than six columns shows the front of the suffix.
A row outside the canvas draws nothing.
.BR tui_yesno_format ()
writes the same row as plain text for
.I cols
columns; it returns the length, or \-1 when
.I outsz
was too small (the text is then cut at a character).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libtuikit (7),
.BR tui_dialog_message (3),
.BR tui_statusline_init (3)
